micro-moments of baseball.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.300
Can you hear the
games through data?

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.460
So yeah, this is
basically just a blog post

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:42.720
that was announced on Thursday,
October 25 by Eric Schmidt,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.465
one of our latest people to join
our team of developer advocates

00:01:46.465 --> 00:01:47.860
for Google Cloud.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.940
And he wrote an
article about how

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.460
to basically try to
understand better

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.760
what goes on in baseball games.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.960
And to do that, he's using
three big data products.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.830
So he's using Cloud Dataflow
to transform the data.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.540
So Cloud Dataflow,
you can imagine it

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.030
as streams of data
going around and kind

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.669
of doing MapReduce-y things.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:10.624
But it is actually
more generic then that.

00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:11.540
It is really powerful.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.910
So have a look at it.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.214
