really cool article.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.590
They've got stuff in here
answering questions like,

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.650
is this pitcher
performing at his best?

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.810
Or what was the probability
of the triple play?

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.370
Which I think is actually kind
of hard questions to answer.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
the cool about this

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.940
is that you basically
want something

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.120
that is kind of real
time because you

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.370
want to be able to
answer questions

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.720
while the game is going on.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.220
So Cloud Dataflow allows you
doing that with streaming.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.260
And then to query all
the data you end up with,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.690
he uses BigQuery.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.690
And to be able to visualize
it in a nice way or to slice

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.872
and get only the piece
of data he cares about,

00:03:04.872 --> 00:03:06.080
he uses Google Cloud Datalab.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.350
And Google Cloud Datalab,
if you have never used it,

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:14.315
it is pretty much like IPython
or Jupyter pages on the cloud.

00:03:14.315 --> 00:03:16.020
It is very, very
useful, especially

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.209
because then you can just say,
run a little MapReduce here.

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:21.000
