00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:36.250
From a high level what is Helm?

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:36.970
What does it do?

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.380
What is it for?

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:41.940
MATTHEW BUTCHER: So Helm is a
package manager for Kubernetes.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:45.670
So Kubernetes is this great
system, comes out at Google,

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:49.230
kind of based on
Google's board project.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:54.270
And it's a great tool for
running applications inside

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.050
of a large-scale cluster.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.080
But one of our early
pain points with it

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.010
was that it's hard to set up
your applications to install

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.534
them into Kubernetes.

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:05.950
So we looked around,
and we looked

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.860
at how we do this in Linux,
how we do it in OS 10.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.390
We looked at Homebrew for OS 10
and Yum and apt-get for Linux.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:15.510
And we said, you know
what would be really cool?

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.830
To build something like
that for Kubernetes that

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.730
made it possible to
express the stuff

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.360
you wanted to run in Kubernetes
as an application package

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.250
that you could share
and reuse and stuff.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:27.520
So at a high level, yeah,
it's a package manager

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.640
designed for Kubernetes.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.760
MARK MANDEL: So what
sort of applications

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.550
would I be looking to deploy?

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.530
Can you give me some examples?

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:36.810
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Yeah.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.610
Actually, I'll let
Michelle answer this.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:40.720
MICHELLE NOORALI: Sure.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.590
You could install a
variety of applications,

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:52.790
WordPress, Jenkins, Databases,
MySQL, [INAUDIBLE], Postgres.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.060
So really, there's
a large range.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.000
MARK MANDEL: So cool.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.660
So I guess that means I'm like,
oh, I need a MySQL database.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.160
I don't have to
work out the details

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.743
of how to set that up and best
practices and things like that.

00:08:02.743 --> 00:08:06.112
I can just go to Helm and be
like, Helm, install MySQL,

00:08:06.112 --> 00:08:07.070
or something like that.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.690
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yeah, you
could grab the Helm binary

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:13.430
and run a search command
and do helm search mysql.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:16.440
And you'll have a
variety of options.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.380
And then you can tweak
your MySQL configuration

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.980
to your preferences
and then run install

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.040
to install the actual chart.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
the question I always

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:31.500
have when I think about these
kind of deployment managers--

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.980
which I really see it as
something that helps you deploy

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:39.490
an application into Kubernetes--
is the default value the best

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:40.320
practice?

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.200
If I just say yes, yes, yes,
yes, yes, I will have something

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.130
that actually works
in production?

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.310
Or is it something
to get started?

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.100
And then you're
going to be tuning up

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.510
the deployment to have something
that matches your use case?

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:54.260
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I
guess I look at them as

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:58.120
like your etc files on Linux.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.570
When you first
install something,

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.940
you want it to
function enough so

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.880
that you've got a starting point
to sort of tweak it from there.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.680
But is the default always
going to be the best?

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:09.470
Probably not.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.810
So the trick is as
chart developers

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.510
is to write these kinds
of Helm chart packages

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:19.500
that are good enough to get
people kind of up and running

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.354
and then help them
understand from there how

00:09:21.354 --> 00:09:22.270
they have to tweak it.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:27.580
Now the additional problem
with cluster-based software

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:31.420
is that when you're installing
something via Homebrew,

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.970
you know you're going to have
one instance running locally

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.660
on exactly one computer
every time you type that in.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:40.360
Well, when you're dealing
with something like a cluster,

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.150
you may want to be running
one instance of Postgres,

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:44.650
for example.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:49.440
Or you may want to be running a
cluster of six Postgres servers

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.890
with a master elected
out of that pool

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.350
or something like that.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.020
So the trick with setting up
defaults, sensible defaults,

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:01.207
is to sort of intuit what the
base case is for most users.

00:10:01.207 --> 00:10:02.790
And then make it
easy enough that they

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.760
can extend it off the base
case with very little effort.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:09.540
MARK MANDEL: So you
use a word there.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.290
You said the word chart.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.530
Michelle, do you want to
explain what a chart is?

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:15.630
What is that?

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.940
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yeah,
so a chart is basically

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.280
a package format for Helm.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.280
It's a directory
structure that consists

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:27.270
of some metadata,
templated or not

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:32.050
templated, Kubernetes resources,
and some documentation.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:36.230
And so applications come
in the form of charts.

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:40.097
And then you install charts
into your cluster using Helm.

00:10:40.097 --> 00:10:41.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Why
are they called charts?

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.390
MICHELLE NOORALI: Oh,
that's a very good question.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.730
Matt Butcher, I'm going
to let you take that one.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:51.070
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Well, so when
we very first started the Helm

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:54.730
project, our first name
for it was actually

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.872
K8S Place, K-8-S Place, which
we thought was kind of cute.

00:10:59.872 --> 00:11:01.080
MICHELLE NOORALI: I loved it.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:01.950
Sorry.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.260
MATTHEW BUTCHER: And the
original name of the package

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.470
was something like K Cubed,
K3 or something like that.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:10.010
It was really pretentious.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.700
And as we got talking about
it, we thought, all right.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:14.520
So we've got this
cute name, K8S Place

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.350
and this pretentious
package name.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.920
And neither of them really
fit the nautical theme

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:23.950
of Kubernetes, where Kubernetes
really means a pilot of a ship.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:26.370
So we hit the
nautical dictionary.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.440
One of the co-founders of the
project, Jack Francis, and I

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.700
hit a nautical
dictionary and sat around

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.090
for about an hour
reading definitions,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.240
trying to come up with
a new name for the tool.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.914
And we eventually alighted on
Helm as the name for the tool.

00:11:39.914 --> 00:11:42.330
And then once we were going
with this nautical dictionary,

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.705
we couldn't let
good enough rest.

00:11:43.705 --> 00:11:46.600
So we decided to
rename the package too.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.640
And we were kind
of thinking, well,

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.270
this is sort of like a
road map or a street map

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:55.180
for how you would install
something into Kubernetes.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:12:02.760
And the CEO of Deis is a big
sailor himself, enjoys boating.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:06.030
And he said, you do not
talk about them as maps.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.410
They're charts, nautical charts.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:09.614
And the name stuck.

00:12:09.614 --> 00:12:11.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
a nice name I like it.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:12.670
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:13.650
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I was
going to say, it's short.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:14.550
It's catchy.

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:19.939
It's relatively direct
as far as conveying

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:21.730
that you're providing
a set of instructions

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:22.830
for how to do something.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:24.930
MARK MANDEL: I guess
that segues kind of

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:29.030
into an interesting thing as
well, the history of Helm.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:30.487
I think there was Helm.

00:12:30.487 --> 00:12:32.070
And then there was
Deployment Manager.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:36.229
And now there's Helm Classic
or they melded and merged.

00:12:36.229 --> 00:12:37.270
Like what happened there?

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:38.540
I didn't quite follow it all.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.998
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Yeah, it's
actually a really cool history.

00:12:40.998 --> 00:12:44.620
It's one you don't get a lot
in the open source world.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:51.240
So a little over a year ago now,
Deis started this Helm project.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.740
It actually came
out of a hackathon.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.430
We were having an "all
hands off site" meeting.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.370
And we set up a hackathon
to try and come up

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:03.080
with some good competitive
projects to build teamwork

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.180
and just enjoy
some time together.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.070
So we formed these
kind of ad hoc teams.

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:09.320
And we were building stuff.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.230
Well, my team built this
little package manager-y thing,

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.590
the K8S Place I was
talking about before.

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:18.390
And the off-site ended.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:19.490
And we all went home.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.170
And it was a Friday afternoon.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:24.720
And I got a call from
the CTO saying, you know,

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.940
that little hackathon
project has some potential.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.750
Maybe we should start
to actually develop it.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:31.817
And so the following
Monday after he got back,

00:13:31.817 --> 00:13:33.900
we formed up a new team
and started going on this.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.950
Now what we didn't
know at the time

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:38.370
was that several Google
engineers were also

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.690
working on a similar
project, which

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:46.710
was a port of the JCP Deployment
Manager project to Kubernetes.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.390
And so for a few months,
we were roughly running

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:50.450
in parity with them.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:54.540
And our emphasis at the time
was the client side experience.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.370
We were trying to build Homebrew
where a lot of the packages

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.070
are just downloaded locally.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.850
And then you're really oriented
around the relationship

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:04.030
between an individual
user and a chart,

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:06.110
which is what we were
calling them at that point.

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:08.400
Whereas, Deployment Manager
was really dealing more

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:12.100
with the process of deployment
and the process of allowing

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.840
a team to together
collaborate on

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:17.580
how something could be deployed
and then updated and maintained

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.910
over its lifetime.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.770
So that was about
October for us last year.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:25.720
Deployment Manager, I think,
had its first public releases

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.440
on November 1, if
I recall correctly.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:33.570
Well, sometime around
January, Google Seattle

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:34.500
invited us to come up.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.760
And the developers of Deployment
Manager sat down with us.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.770
And we talked about
how we might be

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.500
able to do something
that happens very

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:46.380
rarely in the open source world,
which is to merge two projects.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.250
And so we put together a really
rough plan there and sort of

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.450
re-kickstarted the project off.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:54.240
And we all liked the
Helm name because it

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.630
was relatively distinctive.

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:59.040
So we kept that and sort
of rebranded as Helm.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:02.370
And then gradually, what
was the Helm client,

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:03.490
stayed the Helm client.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:05.430
But what was the
Deployment Manager server

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.230
became Tiller, which
is the server side

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.800
component, the cluster side
component of Helm right now.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.730
Well, in the process
of all of this,

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:15.730
well, we had this original
tool that was already

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.860
kind of popular called Helm.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:20.287
And so we rebranded
that one to Helm Classic

00:15:20.287 --> 00:15:22.120
so that people could
still continue using it

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:25.530
while we explored uncharted
waters, to keep playing up

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:26.640
this nautical metaphor.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:27.140
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.140
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
Yeah, there you go.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:29.040
I'm loaded with them.

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.081
MARK MANDEL: Oh, the fun.

00:15:30.081 --> 00:15:31.040
Oh, the fun.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.310
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Yeah.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:37.030
And so Helm Classic was kind
of the original version of it.

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:38.770
And then the Kubernetes
Helm version,

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:40.280
which is in the
Kubernetes project

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:44.070
and is incubated as an official
Kubernetes project, that's

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.230
the one that we're really
moving forward and firing

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.537
full steam ahead on.

00:15:49.537 --> 00:15:50.870
MICHELLE NOORALI: More of those.

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:51.540
More of those.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:51.730
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:52.210
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I know.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:54.460
I'm going to just keep trying
these nautical metaphors

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:56.962
until I run out of them.

00:15:56.962 --> 00:15:58.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I am
looking forward the next.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:01.380
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Me too.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:02.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I had a question

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:05.460
regarding the point of view.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:10.070
So you were talking that Helm
has a deep focus on the user,

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:11.530
on the customer.

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:13.750
But I am curious
about what if I have

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:17.820
an application that I would like
to offer on Helm as a chart?

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.590
What is the process to do that?

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.810
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yeah, so
there's two ways to publish,

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:26.610
or two avenues you can
take to publish your chart.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.530
And the first one is you
can build your own chart

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:30.380
repository.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.730
So charts live in
chart repositories,

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.530
which are just simple
HTTP servers that

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:39.090
follow a particular format.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:43.180
Or you can submit your chart
to the Kubernetes chart

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:44.660
repository.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:48.650
And so if you go to
github.com/kubernetes/charts,

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:53.700
we have the source code for
stable charts and incubator

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.470
charts that people have
submitted over the last few

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:59.950
months to share
with the community.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:04.460
And so if you have a chart
that you-- is working

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:05.960
and you want to get
feedback on, you

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:10.160
might submit it to the
incubator chart repository.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:15.829
And if you have more of a
production-ready chart that

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:17.770
meets deep technical
requirements,

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:20.960
then you would submit it to
the stable chart repository.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.650
MARK MANDEL: So what
actually goes into a chart?

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:25.150
Can you go a little--
if I'm writing

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.700
one, is there a template
language or something?

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:28.540
What's the--

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:29.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Is it a YAML file?

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:30.410
MARK MANDEL: Is it a YAML file?

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:30.720
Yeah.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:31.410
What's the deal?

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.645
MATTHEW BUTCHER: It's
everything a YAML file now?

00:17:33.645 --> 00:17:35.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, hopefully.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:35.410
MARK MANDEL: Hopefully.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Not JSON anymore.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:39.228
MICHELLE NOORALI: I'm
going to let Butcher take

00:17:39.228 --> 00:17:40.656
that one because he developed--

00:17:40.656 --> 00:17:41.590
MARK MANDEL: Oh, fair enough.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:42.560
MICHELLE NOORALI: --all of that.

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.030
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I was going
to let you take that one so I

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:45.119
didn't have to answer it.

00:17:45.119 --> 00:17:46.410
MICHELLE NOORALI: I don't mind.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:50.619
MATTHEW BUTCHER: So a chart is
a bundle of plain text files,

00:17:50.619 --> 00:17:51.660
really for the most part.

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:53.490
It references a
Docker image that's

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:55.330
got to be pushed to
a registry somewhere,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.040
or could reference a
Docker image, I should say.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:01.025
But really, it's got
a control manifest.

00:18:01.025 --> 00:18:04.930
It sort of tells you information
about the chart, readme file

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.570
and notes file that tell you
kind of how to use the chart.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.910
And then for the most part,
the real meat of the chart

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:13.229
is a set of YAML templates.

00:18:13.229 --> 00:18:13.770
There you go.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.630
There's the YAML.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.560
We use the Go template
language to be

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:21.025
able to populate YAML files.

00:18:21.025 --> 00:18:23.650
And then we can upload the YAML
files directly into Kubernetes.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:27.460
And Kubernetes understands
those files natively.

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:29.900
So the Go template language
has been pretty nice for us

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:33.710
because it compiles very
well and usually gives

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.160
relatively helpful
error messages

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.640
and is very fast for us.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.400
And we can sandbox
it very efficiently

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.130
without having to worry about
breakout conditions and things.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:47.250
But you know, your typical
chart runs probably

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.410
four templates and then
the chart.YAML control file

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:53.540
and then a couple of text
files to explain how to use it.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:55.770
Oh, and actually in
order to kind of get

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:58.810
started with it-- because I know
you lead with that question--

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.710
we originally realized that--
or we realized very quickly

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:05.204
that it's a pain to keep writing
YAML files over and over again.

00:19:05.204 --> 00:19:06.620
And on top of that,
that there are

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.050
a lot of patterns
that really are

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.450
common across many, many charts.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:14.540
So we built a command called
Helm Create that will allow

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:15.690
you to create a new chart.

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.820
So you can type in
Helm Create WordPress.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.870
And you'll have a stubbed
out chart ready to go.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:22.840
And then you can just
modify it from there

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:25.300
instead of having to
start afresh each time.

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:26.050
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:26.549
All right.

00:19:26.549 --> 00:19:29.430
So say I've written
my chart or I've

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:32.360
gone to look at, what is it,
the stable or the incubating

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:33.200
charts.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:34.620
And I want to install one.

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:37.210
What does that look like?

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.880
MICHELLE NOORALI: So
when you install the Helm

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.980
tool, by default we give you
access to the stable charts.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:48.850
So you can simply do a helm
search on whatever keyword.

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:51.860
Or you can do a helm search and
pass in the repository name.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:55.710
And you'll see all of the
charts that are available.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.820
And then if you want to add,
say, your friend's chart

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:01.300
repository to search
through those as well,

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:06.320
then you can run a helm repo add
command to add that repository.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:08.524
And then you can search
through those as well.

00:20:08.524 --> 00:20:10.440
MARK MANDEL: And can I
pass variables through?

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:12.523
I know you said it was a
sort of templating thing.

00:20:12.523 --> 00:20:14.480
What sort of controls
do I have there?

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:15.980
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
Yeah, so by default

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:18.080
when you're setting
up your templates,

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:20.140
one of the files that's
good to provide with it

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:23.390
is sort of a default
values file, which

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:25.840
is called values.YAML
because we're really

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.570
into our YAML files.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:30.060
And the values.YAML
is basically,

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:34.390
you can think of it sort of like
as the etc file for your chart.

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:39.950
It contains YAML data that
gets pushed by default

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:41.200
through the template engine.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:43.940
But more than that, it's
sort of like a way for us

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:46.737
to expose to
developers, hey, here

00:20:46.737 --> 00:20:49.320
are the different little options
that you can tweak and change

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.620
easily with this chart by just
passing in your own custom YAML

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:55.050
file or your own
custom YAML values when

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:56.530
you run the installer.

00:20:56.530 --> 00:21:00.750
So say I've got my
WordPress chart.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.830
And I want to
create my admin user

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:05.110
account when I do the install.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.990
I don't want the default
password for that account.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.030
So when I type in the
helm install command,

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:13.830
I can either give it a YAML
overrides file that will

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.000
override the configuration
for that and maybe set

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.840
username:mbutcher for that.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:22.530
Or I can use the set
flag on the command line

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:26.129
and set very particular
configuration parameters.

00:21:26.129 --> 00:21:27.920
But in either case,
really what we're doing

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:30.880
is we're allowing the
end-user, the person who's

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:34.320
consuming the chart,
to be able to specify

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.990
some of their own configuration
custom right from the get-go.

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:38.740
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:39.239
All right.

00:21:39.239 --> 00:21:40.130
I got a fun question.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.205
If I do a helm install,
is it idempotent?

00:21:44.439 --> 00:21:46.480
Yeah, if I'm trying to
set up some infrastructure

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:47.690
and I'm automating
it and I just want

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:49.981
to keep running it over and
over again, is that the way

00:21:49.981 --> 00:21:50.870
it works?

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:52.990
MATTHEW BUTCHER: So we
actually-- originally,

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:55.380
we didn't do it that way.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:59.370
But over time, it has
been more and more

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:02.620
popular to be able to deploy
Helm charts via CI/CD.

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:07.050
And so we have introduced,
first of all, a renewed effort

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:10.210
to try and make them
more idempotent and also

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:11.790
added some additional
flags in there

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.415
that will allow you
to run one command.

00:22:13.415 --> 00:22:17.740
So helm install will put
your chart into the cluster.

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:19.970
And it'll start tracking
that chart over time.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.920
So you can recover
history and so on.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.670
And then helm update
is used to kind

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:27.690
of re-release over and
over or release updates.

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:29.190
You change a
configuration value.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:29.940
You run an update.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:33.500
You go from version
1.2.3 to 1.3.4.

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:34.490
You run an update.

00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:37.300
But in order to make it possible
to run it through a CI/CD

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.250
system over and over
again without having

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.370
to query the cluster
first, we just

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.200
added some flags onto the
update command that basically,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:48.590
if you do helm update --install,
it tells update, hey, try

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:49.350
and update this.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.954
But if it already exists,
just install it first.

00:22:51.954 --> 00:22:53.370
MARK MANDEL: If
it already exists?

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:53.910
MATTHEW BUTCHER: If it doesn't--

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:54.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
If it doesn't exit.

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:55.480
MATTHEW BUTCHER: Did
I get it backwards?

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:55.980
Yeah.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.280
I got it backwards.

00:22:57.280 --> 00:22:59.020
Install it if it doesn't exist.

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:00.061
Update it if it does.

00:23:00.061 --> 00:23:00.560
There we go.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.960
Yeah, I know my-- I can speak.

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:04.960
MICHELLE NOORALI: Also,
that command is upgrade.

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:06.100
So just-- update.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:07.110
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
And I said update.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:07.610
Didn't I?

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:08.380
Yeah.

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:09.046
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:09.046 --> 00:23:10.120
So helm upgrade install.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:10.920
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yes.

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:11.586
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:11.586 --> 00:23:12.310
Cool.

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:13.893
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I have a question.

00:23:13.893 --> 00:23:16.030
Before we were talking
about Helm as a way

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:18.370
for application
developers basically

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:21.035
to provide an easy way to
deploy their application

00:23:21.035 --> 00:23:22.480
onto Kubernetes.

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.320
But now you're
talking about CI/CD.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:25.580
It makes me think.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:27.790
Is it also a good
tool for someone

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:29.310
like a company
that they're using

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:32.580
their own system to deploy it
on Kubernetes through CI/CD?

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:35.550
Could that be easier than
just using Kubernetes files,

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:37.920
just playing Kubernetes
config files?

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:40.590
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I
think that the way that

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:45.390
CI/CD systems get used
is as sort of a-- well,

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:48.120
for sure kind of last mile
piece of your equation.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:51.197
So as a developer, I need
to build my application.

00:23:51.197 --> 00:23:53.530
Then I want to be able to
test it locally and figure out

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:54.920
how it's going to work.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.690
Then I want to contribute
it up to the repo.

00:23:57.690 --> 00:24:01.260
And then the CI/CD system
takes over from there.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:04.200
And what Helm gives you-- the
advantage that Helm really

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:07.559
confers in this process over,
say, just raw manifest files,

00:24:07.559 --> 00:24:08.600
actually there's several.

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:12.910
But the first one is that
it gives the developer

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:15.700
that opportunity to fully
test out and work together--

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.600
work this whole thing together,
share it with other developers

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:21.240
and things like that prior
to it ever getting deployed.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.420
But then once we get into
the release pipeline,

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.030
this is where a
chart ends up being

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:28.690
very powerful because
a chart is basically

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.740
immutable from the
time you package it up

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.580
where it can get tracked, first
of all, inside of the cluster

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:38.450
but also in these chart
repositories elsewhere.

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:42.240
And so I can-- if I'm pushing
things into repositories

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.380
and then pulling
them out in CI/CD,

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:48.920
I've got the exact records in
the repository of what thing

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:49.700
I pulled out.

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:52.780
And I can pull it out,
rebuild it, and so on.

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:55.290
I've seen a lot of cases
where people very successfully

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:59.020
use just raw Kubernetes manifest
files in their CI/CD system.

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:01.650
But where it becomes problematic
for them is in the development

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.000
process, where
oftentimes, we end up--

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.630
we fall back to the old school
model of throwing applications

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:10.390
over the wall, where developers
develop the application.

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:12.984
And another team is responsible
for-- the DevOps team

00:25:12.984 --> 00:25:15.400
is responsible for packaging
that and then somehow pushing

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:16.600
it out to the cluster.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:18.430
But then when you come
up with those bugs

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:20.740
that seem to
inevitably arise where

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:23.130
something fails as it
goes through the staging

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:24.290
or production process.

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.287
And the developer simply says,
hey, I only wrote the binary.

00:25:27.287 --> 00:25:28.620
The rest of that stuff is yours.

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:31.210
And then there's this
long back and forth over

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:34.590
whose responsibility it is and
what your particular compliance

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:37.190
regulations say you can see
about the production logs

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:40.860
versus, and-- We feel
like by packaging things

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.830
just like Debian and
Fedora and all of those

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:45.280
have done for
years and years, we

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:47.060
gain all those
advantages of being

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:49.065
able to reproduce
locally, reproduce

00:25:49.065 --> 00:25:50.940
the same exact thing in
a testing environment

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:52.250
and then in production.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:53.990
MARK MANDEL: I guess that
makes a lot of sense too.

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:55.656
It kind of solves
that problem that I've

00:25:55.656 --> 00:25:58.346
seen where locally I want to
have two instances of this

00:25:58.346 --> 00:26:00.470
and two instances of that
and one instance of that.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:02.553
But then in production, I
want to have 300 of this

00:26:02.553 --> 00:26:03.670
and 300 of that.

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:06.270
And so now you can-- maybe
your defaults are two,

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:08.100
but you're production
environment variables

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:08.900
are at 300.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So my next question

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.809
is, can I deploy to MINICUBE?

00:26:13.809 --> 00:26:15.100
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yes, you can.

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:15.837
Absolutely.

00:26:15.837 --> 00:26:16.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:18.720
That makes for a very
interesting development

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:19.220
experience.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:19.450
I guess.

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:20.320
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I
really want to try this.

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:22.000
This actually is just
really, really cool.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.458
MATTHEW BUTCHER: I think about
half of the development team

00:26:24.458 --> 00:26:27.244
uses MINICUBE for their
development process.

00:26:27.244 --> 00:26:28.660
We try and mix it
up a little bit.

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:31.140
But honestly, the barrier
to entry for MINICUBE

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:34.190
is so low that we all
sort of favor it and don't

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.940
like trying to set
up local Kubernetes

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.850
clusters from scratch
or anything like that.

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:42.490
But between MINICUBE and
GKE, the Kubernetes world

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:45.184
is just way too easy
to get going on now.

00:26:45.184 --> 00:26:46.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:47.891
MARK MANDEL: I want to
talk about-- I think

00:26:47.891 --> 00:26:50.540
it's really interesting and
a fairly important thing too,

00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:53.390
I believe you can sign
charts so they're verified

00:26:53.390 --> 00:26:54.485
from where they come from.

00:26:54.485 --> 00:26:56.510
Can one of you tell us a
little bit more about that?

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:57.490
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
Yeah, Michelle is

00:26:57.490 --> 00:27:00.130
going to point at me because
this is one of the things

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:02.980
that I have been
rather insistent on.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:09.000
It is often the case
that we forget about some

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.770
of the security
measures to begin with.

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:14.680
And then we try and
retrofit them back in later.

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:16.490
And we're always
afraid of doing that.

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:17.570
I'm sure we're
going to have to do

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:18.860
that in some areas or another.

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:21.560
But one of the things we
decided we wanted to not

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:24.850
overlook at the beginning
was the trust factor

00:27:24.850 --> 00:27:26.965
when it came to packages.

00:27:26.965 --> 00:27:29.340
So in thinking through this
thing, we thought, all right.

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:31.150
Well, we want to
make it so that there

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.490
are ways to establish
trust where you can say,

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.220
I know this package
came from so and so.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.170
And I know that it hasn't
been modified since then.

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:40.910
But on the flipside,
we wanted to make it

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:44.240
so that people who were not--
who were in the development

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:47.760
cycle or who didn't particularly
care about that kind of thing

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:49.470
didn't have to use it.

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.910
And so we came up
with a model that

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:55.500
allows for signing
of packages and also

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:59.330
allows you to sort of optionally
enable this kind of workflow

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:01.950
so that it becomes sort
of standard for you.

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:06.280
So the way we modeled it was
really after Debian's model

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:09.260
with a little bit of
looking at Yum and the way

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:11.700
that Red Hat has done
it for a long time.

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:14.790
And we sign packages
with PGP keys

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:17.160
so that we can
establish some identity.

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:22.542
And the clear sign block
contains the SHA256SUMS

00:28:22.542 --> 00:28:24.250
of the files that are
included so you can

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:25.416
verify those sort of things.

00:28:25.416 --> 00:28:28.730
Very, very, very traditional,
uninspired someone

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:30.749
once told me in regard to this.

00:28:30.749 --> 00:28:31.790
MARK MANDEL: That's fine.

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:32.290
MATTHEW BUTCHER: And
I went, well, OK.

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:34.864
Well, uninspired is OK
in security, I think.

00:28:34.864 --> 00:28:35.573
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:28:35.573 --> 00:28:36.989
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
So you can choose

00:28:36.989 --> 00:28:38.510
to sign your charts if you want.

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:40.830
And then you can choose
to verify your charts when

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:42.930
you install them if you want.

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:44.750
And then more
recently, we've been

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:46.490
working on sort of
integrating this tool

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:48.099
chain with things like Keybase.

00:28:48.099 --> 00:28:49.890
I'm really excited
about Keybase because it

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.830
makes public key
cryptography sort

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:57.270
of easy for non-cryptographers,
easy to gain trust

00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:03.265
on your identity, easy
to integrate your GPG

00:29:03.265 --> 00:29:07.560
key with a reliable key signing
authority, stuff like that.

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:09.930
And so we've been working
a little bit on that.

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:12.560
But then at the end
of the day, we've

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:15.450
been trying to figure
out sort of how--

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:17.690
now that we've got
these primitives-- how

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:22.170
do organizations want to be
able to use this technology?

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:25.280
And so we've tried to
keep it unopinionated

00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:28.350
but then be able to give people
some guidance in how you should

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.250
issue your keys and how you
should sign your packages

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:33.270
and how you should tell
people how to do that.

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:35.186
That's going to be an
ongoing challenge for us

00:29:35.186 --> 00:29:37.004
up through the 2.0
release, I think.

00:29:37.004 --> 00:29:37.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:41.510
So since we're talking about
2.0 releases and the future,

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:44.440
why don't you-- could
you share something

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:47.460
that you're very excited
about, the roadmap?

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:51.600
MICHELLE NOORALI: Being able
to test your charts once you've

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:54.360
installed them automatically,
that is something

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.400
that I'm super pumped about.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:00.050
So you can install
your chart and then run

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:07.240
a test command that has some
pre-defined definitions of what

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:11.840
it means for success-- or
for a successful installation

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:12.430
of your chart.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.360
Being able to do that
in an automated way

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:16.364
is something that I'm
very pumped about.

00:30:16.364 --> 00:30:17.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:19.619
What about you, Matt?

00:30:19.619 --> 00:30:21.160
MATTHEW BUTCHER:
I'm honestly looking

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:24.937
forward to hitting the
stability marker because it'll

00:30:24.937 --> 00:30:27.270
give me a little bit of a
break from implementing stuff.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:32.507
But I think other than testing,
which I am really, really

00:30:32.507 --> 00:30:33.090
excited about.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:34.756
Michelle's plan for
that is really cool.

00:30:34.756 --> 00:30:37.570
I think that what I'm
kind of looking forward to

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:39.660
is getting to focus
a little bit more

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:42.760
on the developer experience
for the person who's actually

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:46.030
writing the code that's
going to get packaged up

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:47.960
into these applications.

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:52.250
Because as a Python
developer, it

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:54.820
would be nice to be able to have
some tools, even if they are

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:58.100
Metatools that sit on top of
Helm that will help me get

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:00.680
from my little
block of Python code

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:05.600
to a containerized application
with a chart scaffolding around

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:08.570
it that I can deploy with
the helm install command.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:09.320
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:11.236
Well, we are running out
of time a little bit.

00:31:11.236 --> 00:31:12.999
But I do want to
touch on one thing.

00:31:12.999 --> 00:31:14.790
So if people want to
get involved with Helm

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:17.520
or they want to try Helm,
what are the best resources?

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:18.660
Where should people go?

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:21.118
MICHELLE NOORALI: Yeah, the
GitHub repository is definitely

00:31:21.118 --> 00:31:22.660
the best place to start.

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:25.640
And all of their released
binaries are there.

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:30.040
You can use Homebrew to
download Helm and install Helm.

