00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:29.220
So the question
of the week today

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:32.570
is about Docker
images in the cloud.

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:37.350
And basically, it is,
OK, so I have an image

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:39.007
that I want to build regularly.

00:35:39.007 --> 00:35:41.090
So imagine that, for
instance, you have something.

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:41.881
You have a project.

00:35:41.881 --> 00:35:44.020
And every single time you
have a comment on GitHub,

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:46.100
you want to create a new image.

00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:48.300
And there's ways of doing this.

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:51.877
But is there a Google Cloud
Platform way of doing it?

00:35:51.877 --> 00:35:54.460
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so there is
totally a Google Cloud Platform

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:55.450
way of doing it.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:57.540
We have this service,
it's currently in beta,

00:35:57.540 --> 00:36:00.310
called Google Cloud
Container Builder.

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:02.850
It is literally exactly
what you described it as.

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:05.950
It's a REST API that you can
send a series of commands to.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:08.790
And the final output is
a Docker container image,

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:12.840
which then gets served up to
gcr.io, our Google Container

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:14.120
Registry.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:15.950
And so it's a really
nice way of if you're

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:17.880
using Google
Container's Registries

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:19.490
and you have like
a CI or CD system

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:21.781
or some sort of automation
for building these container

00:36:21.781 --> 00:36:24.082
images, it's an easy way
of basically saying, hey,

00:36:24.082 --> 00:36:25.540
I would like to
build one of those.

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:29.530
Push that up into the
Container Registry.

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:31.350
And then I want to
serve that maybe

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:34.090
to Container Engine,
Compute Engine, App Engine,

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:36.100
Flexible Environment,
anywhere from there.

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:38.683
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I think
that it is a pretty interesting

00:36:38.683 --> 00:36:41.660
thing to mention a little
bit about Google Container

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:42.660
Registry.

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.960
So Google Container
Registry, for those

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:46.850
that might have
never heard about it,

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:48.470
it is just a Container Registry.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.480
So nothing really
fancy in there.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:51.730
But it is private.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:55.010
So only your project, your
Google Cloud Platform project

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:56.280
has access to it.

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:59.950
So it basically removes
all the issues on, oh, I

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:02.110
want to have a Docker image.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:04.110
And I want to be able to
pull it from somewhere.

00:37:04.110 --> 00:37:05.360
But I want to make it public.

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:06.860
I don't want other
people to see it.

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:08.750
So it is a very good
solution for that.

00:37:08.750 --> 00:37:09.640
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
and of course,

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:11.920
that also ties into all of
our security stuff as well.

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:13.440
So you already know it's secure.

00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:15.160
And you can control who
accesses it and whatnot.

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:16.180
So it's pretty awesome.

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:18.304
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And it's
also in the same network.

00:37:18.304 --> 00:37:20.580
So the deployment will be
